-- title:
--   Detector functionals of retained tree nodes
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals. The tree is built level by level from candidate records: each candidate is a generated map attached to a parent node together with a norm-one functional chosen after the map, and every candidate, including those that later receive coefficient zero, consumes a global ordinal $n$ with budget $2^{-(n+9)}$. For a retained node $v$ at level $k+1$ with label $\ell(v)$ and functional $\varphi_v$:
--
--   1. **Budget.** The budget fixed before selection equals the retained budget $\beta(\ell(v))=2^{-(\ell(v)+9)}$.
--   2. **Norm.** $\|\varphi_v\|\le1$.
--   3. **Detection.** For every $z$ in the detector set of the prefix of $v$ (all prediction limits and the dense points $z_0,\dots,z_k$),
--   $$
--   \varphi_v(g_v(a)-z)\ge1-\beta(\ell(v)).
--   $$
--   4. **Path displacement.** The proper ancestors of $v$ move $g_v(a)$ by a total of at most $\beta(\ell(v))$.
